==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|group-think}}

===Etymology===
{{coin|en|William H. Whyte|in=1952|nobycat=1}}, from {{compound|en|group|think}}, modelled on earlier {{m|en|doublethink}} from Orwell's ''Nineteen Eighty-Four''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɹuːpθɪŋk/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A process of [[reasoning]] or [[decision]]-[[making]] by a [[group]], especially one characterized by [[uncritical]] [[acceptance]] of or [[conformity]] to a [[perceived]] [[majority]] [[view]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Irving Janis|Irving L. Janis]]|title=Groupthink in Washington|work=The New York Times|date=1973-05-28|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1973/05/28/archives/groupthink-in-washington.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=At present we do not know what percentage of all national fiascoes are attributable to '''groupthink'''. Some decisions of poor quality that turn out to be fiascoes might be ascribed primarily to mistakes made by just one man, the Chief Executive. Others arise because of a faulty policy formulated by a group of executives whose decision‐making procedures were impaired by errors having little or nothing to do with '''groupthink'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=July 12, 2005|author=w:Jacob Weisberg|url=https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2005/07/the-case-for-outing-the-plame-leakers.html|work=Slate Magazine|title=The Anonymity Trap|passage=This gang-bang speaks more to journalistic '''groupthink''' than to any real moral or legal reasoning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=w:Christopher Clark|title=The Sleepwalkers|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2013|page=395|passage=A militant '''group-think''' seized hold of the ministry.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|herdthink}}
* {{l|en|echo chamber}}

